<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

	<PropertyGroup>
		<!-- The floor and the newest, and nothing between: a parser here is ordinary C#
		     over a generated one, so netstandard2.0 is what every consumer can read and
		     net10.0 is what a consumer on the newest runtime gets without the shims. -->
		<TargetFrameworks>netstandard2.0;net10.0</TargetFrameworks>
		<RootNamespace>DotGram.Sql</RootNamespace>

		<Description>SQL parsers written in .gram: ISO SQL:2023, with a tree of the standard's own constructs, and SQL-92 and Microsoft's T-SQL, which build one tree of records between them.</Description>
		<PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
		<PackageTags>parser;sql;iso-sql;sql-2023;sql-92;tsql;t-sql;sql-server;dotgram</PackageTags>
		<PackageReleaseNotes>Since 0.2.0. A script is read the way a client tool reads it, outside the T-SQL grammar, and in a text sent in one call `GO` is a word, as the server reads it. Both trees compare by content: a tree equals its own reparse, and every list a node holds is a SqlList&lt;T&gt; rather than an array or an IReadOnlyList&lt;T&gt;.

New: SqlList&lt;T&gt;, in DotGram.Sql, the list every node holds: a read-only struct over one array, the size of the reference it replaces, compared element by element and hashed over its length and every element in order, the same on every framework. It reads as an array does — Length and Count, an indexer, foreach without allocating, AsSpan, Slice and list patterns, Exists and TrueForAll — and is an IReadOnlyList&lt;T&gt;. default is the empty list and equals []; a member a record may leave out is a SqlList&lt;T&gt;?, where null is not empty. It is made with a collection expression ([a, b], [.. items]) or SqlList.From(items) and items.ToSqlList(), all of which copy; there is no conversion from an array. Equality and GetHashCode of a node of either tree throw InsufficientExecutionStackException on a tree deeper than the stack has room for, where they would have overflowed it.

New: SqlScript.Read(text, options), in DotGram.Sql, cuts a script into the batches a client tool sends: ScriptProfile.SqlCmd() reads separator lines, GO n, :setvar and $(name) substitution, :r through ScriptOptions.ResolveInclude and every other command as a ScriptDirective that is reported and not run, with ScriptOptions.MaximumIncludes (10,000 unless said) bounding how many files :r reads in all, a guard sqlcmd does not have, for scripts that are not trusted; ScriptProfile.Ssms() reads separator lines and nothing else; either takes another separator word. It is held byte for byte to what ODBC sqlcmd 18.6 sends the server. Each ScriptBatch says how many times it is sent (Count), whether it is a window of the script itself (IsVerbatim) and where any position in it was written (Locate); SqlScript.Diagnostics says what sqlcmd would have said, and HasClientSyntax whether anything in the text was the tool's rather than the server's. TransactSqlParser.TryParseSql(ScriptBatch), with its quiet TryParseSql(ScriptBatch, out Statement[]) and throwing ParseSql(ScriptBatch) forms and their levels, plain and Located, read one batch and refuse a reading that stops before the batch's end.  Batch gains Source, the ScriptBatch it was read from. TransactSqlParser.Located is the same parser as TransactSqlParser called with locations on, rather than a second one compiled from the grammar, so the assembly carries one T-SQL parser instead of two. A located node's SqlSpan also says where the text in front of it begins (GapStart: the end of the token before it, or where the reading began), so a comment in front of a node is found by reading that gap alone, and a T-SQL match says where its reading stopped looking (ReadingEnd).

Changed (breaking): every array member of the T-SQL tree (SqlSyntax.cs) is a SqlList&lt;T&gt;, and every T[]? a SqlList&lt;T&gt;?: 79 under Statement, 30 under Clause, 9 under TableReference, 7 under Query, 6 under Expression, and Batch.Statements; Statement.None, Expression.None, TableReference.None and Clause.None are empty SqlLists, as is Syntax.NoNames. Every IReadOnlyList&lt;T&gt; member of the SQL:2023 tree (DotGram.Sql.Ast) is a SqlList&lt;T&gt;, 183 in all: 38 under Statement, 15 under Expression, and the rest one to four to a family — TableSource, GroupingElement, GrantBody, RevokeBody, RowPattern, RowPatternClause, TableContents, DataType, Constraint, the JSON, routine, type, transform, descriptor and privilege nodes among them — and SetSessionCharacteristics.Characteristics is a SqlList&lt;SqlList&lt;TransactionMode&gt;&gt;. Reading code mostly compiles unchanged (foreach, an indexer, Length, Count, LINQ, list patterns). What breaks: handing an array where a list is taken — write [.. array] or SqlList.From(array); Array.Exists, Array.TrueForAll and other T[] methods on a member — use the list's own Exists and TrueForAll, or AsSpan; assigning a List&lt;T&gt; or an array to a SQL:2023 init property; array covariance, a SqlList&lt;Derived&gt; not being a SqlList&lt;Base&gt;; and writing into an element. The factories that build a node from a list take it as a SqlList&lt;T&gt;? where they took an array, so that a caller keeps no handle into the node: Statement.Commanded (the list form), Defined, Holding, OfDatabase, Altered, Flagged and Dropped; TableReference.Joining; Clause.Constrained, Keyed, Indexed and Ordered; Syntax.Referenced, Preceded, Sourced and Cursor. Syntax.HasOption takes a ReadOnlySpan&lt;Clause&gt;. QualifiedName.Of copies the identifiers it is given. Batch.Source takes no part in a batch's equality, as a span takes none in a node's, so two readings of one script give equal batches. Trees compare by content: two readings of one text are equal and hash alike where an array member made them unequal, two equal subtrees in different places are equal, so a table keyed by node has to be keyed by reference, and hash values differ from before. A node's span takes no part in the equality of the SQL:2023 tree either: a located tree equals the plain one.

Changed: TransactSqlParser is carried immediately (Carrier = GramCarrier.Immediate): a node is built the moment its rule is read, with no tape recorded during the parse and replayed after it, which makes every T-SQL reading faster and allocates more only on a text that is refused part-way, where the nodes of the abandoned reading were built for nothing. Trees, refusals and messages are the same as before, through the plain door and the located one. The factories of the T-SQL tree read nothing outside their arguments, so a derivation the parse gives up leaves nothing behind.

Changed: A located node's span is the range of the rule that built it, and a clause's keyword is no longer inside the value it introduces: a WHERE, HAVING or ON condition, an ELSE statement, an OVER window, an option's value and the like now span their own text where they used to include the keyword in front of them, and a member chain or AT TIME ZONE spans the whole of what it stands on. A node's Span no longer takes part in the equality of the T-SQL tree (SqlSyntax.cs): a located tree equals the plain tree of the same text, and two equal subtrees written in different places compare equal, so a table keyed by node has to be keyed by reference. A copy of a located node made with `with` keeps its span and the gap in front of it and says it is stale (SqlSpan.IsStale). ParseScript, TryParseScript and their levels keep their names and their three remaining forms and now cut a script as sqlcmd cuts it, so they answer differently wherever the old reading disagreed with sqlcmd. `GO;` is text and not a separator, and so are a GO after a statement on the same line (`PRINT 1 GO`) and a GO after a lone carriage return; the server then reads the GO as a word. A block comment does not nest for the cut, so `/* /* x */` and a GO line end a batch inside what the server reads as one comment. `GO /* c */`, `GO 2;` and `GO x` are refused at that line, as sqlcmd stops there. A last GO with no line break after it ends the batch rather than being read by its last statement as a name. A GO with nothing before it ends no batch, so no empty Batch comes back for it, and a batch of only spacing or comments — after the last GO, say — is a Batch with no statements, since sqlcmd sends it. :setvar lines are read and $(name) substituted where they used to be refused, and a :r is refused, there being no file to read. In a batch where a variable was substituted, spans are positions in Batch.Source.Text, which Batch.Source.Locate maps back to the script. The Match forms build a refusal's message when they refuse, where they used to build it when Error was first read. ParseSql no longer refuses a GO line: in one call GO is a word — `SELECT 1` and a line `GO` is a column called GO, a lone `GO` a call to a procedure of that name — as the engine reads it. Text from a file or an editor is a script, for ParseScript; text a program sends in one command is for ParseSql.

Removed: the four position and window forms of TryParseScript — (string, int), (string, ref int, out Batch[]), (string, int, int) and (string, ref int, int, out Batch[]) — at every level from TryParseScript to TryParseScript170, on TransactSqlParser and on TransactSqlParser.Located, 72 methods: cut the text with SqlScript.Read and read each batch with TryParseSql(ScriptBatch), whose positions are the script's. Syntax.Scripted and Syntax.GoLine, the helpers of the grammar rules that cut a script, which are gone with them: SqlScript.Read does the cutting, and ScriptBatch.Count is the count a GO line wrote.

Changed (breaking): SqlStandardParser reads by level, six entries where it published forty-two productions: ParseValue (a literal, signed numbers among them, or NULL), ParseDataType, ParseExpression (was ParseValueExpression), ParseSearchCondition, ParseStatement (one statement of any kind, the semicolon optional; it replaces ParseDirectSQLStatement, ParseSQLProcedureStatement and ParseQueryExpression — a query reads as a statement, to the same Statement.Select, whose Updatability says whether FOR UPDATE or FOR READ ONLY followed it) and ParseSql (statements, each ended by a semicolon or by the end of the text, as a Statement[]; an empty statement is refused). Each has its TryParse forms. ParseLiteral is ParseValue, which reads NULL as well (LiteralValue.Null); a refusal of ParseValue lists "NULL"i among what was expected. A production below the levels is read through the level that holds it — a table reference as a query that names it, a predicate as a search condition — and a statement's kind is the type of its node. TransactSqlParser is unchanged: its ParseValueExpression is an expression, where SqlStandardParser.ParseValue is a literal.

Changed: SqlStandardParser is carried immediately (Carrier = GramCarrier.Immediate): a node is built the moment its rule is read, with no tape recorded during the parse and replayed after it. Every reading through the six entries is faster, and more is allocated only on a text refused part-way, where the nodes of the abandoned reading were built for nothing. Trees, refusals and messages are the same as before. The factories of the SQL:2023 tree read nothing outside their arguments and the guards read a tower's roles, so a derivation the parse gives up leaves nothing behind.

Changed: Sql92Parser is carried immediately too (Carrier = GramCarrier.Immediate), the last of the three parsers here to be: a node is built the moment its rule is read, with no tape recorded during the parse and replayed after it. Every accepted reading through its four entries is faster; a text refused part-way allocates more, where the nodes of the abandoned reading were built for nothing, and one refused late, after a long accepted prefix, can take longer than it did on the tape. Trees, refusals and messages are the same as before. The factories of the tree in SqlSyntax.cs read nothing outside their arguments, so a derivation the parse gives up leaves nothing behind.

Removed: from SqlStandardParser, the Parse and TryParse forms of UnsignedLiteral, Identifier, IdentifierChain, ColumnReference, TableName, SchemaName, CommonValueExpression, NumericValueExpression, StringValueExpression, CharacterValueExpression, BinaryValueExpression, DatetimeValueExpression, IntervalValueExpression, BooleanValueExpression, Predicate, RowValuePredicand, ValueExpressionPrimary, QueryExpression, QuerySpecification, TableReference, InsertStatement, UpdateStatementSearched, UpdateStatementPositioned, DeleteStatementSearched, DeleteStatementPositioned, MergeStatement, TruncateTableStatement, SQLSchemaStatement, DirectSQLStatement, SQLTransactionStatement, SQLConnectionStatement, SQLSessionStatement, SQLDiagnosticsStatement, DirectSQLDataStatement, SQLControlStatement, SQLDataStatement, SQLDynamicStatement, SQLProcedureStatement: 258 public methods. ParseLiteral and ParseValueExpression are renamed ParseValue and ParseExpression, and the class publishes 42 methods where it published 286.

Fixed: ten thousand T-SQL CASE … ELSE CASE … ended the process with a stack overflow in the generated reader, and are read. SqlWriter and Sql2023Writer write every tree a parser gives back, carrying the walk onto a thread of their own where the stack runs low, where a chain of about ten thousand + overflowed, and so did nested GROUPING SETS and JSON_TABLE NESTED PATH in Sql2023Writer; and the T-SQL reading's checks of a chain of UNION, of brackets and of PIVOT loop rather than recurse, where ten thousand UNION overflowed a thread of a megabyte. Equality and GetHashCode still throw InsufficientExecutionStackException on a tree deeper than the stack has room for.</PackageReleaseNotes>

		<!-- What a caller reads in the editor. The other packages here fail a build on a public
		     member without a comment; this one cannot yet, because the tree that is leaving has four
		     hundred records and none of them is documented (docs/design/sql-tsql-tree.md). So the
		     documentation ships and CS1591 is off: a node written from here on carries its summary and
		     the page of the specification it comes from, and the day the count reaches zero the NoWarn
		     goes and the compiler keeps it at zero. Turning it on now rather than then is what makes
		     the new nodes visible to IntelliSense while they are being written. -->
		<GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
		<!-- The two that say a comment is MISSING: CS1591 for a public member without one, CS1573 for
		     a record that documents some of its parameters and not the rest. Both are about coverage,
		     and both are the old tree's. The ones that say a comment is WRONG stay on: CS1570 for
		     malformed XML, CS1574 for a cref that resolves to nothing, because those are defects in
		     what a reader is told rather than gaps in what they are told. Turning the documentation on
		     found six of CS1570 in the summaries of Sql2023Ast.cs, where a BNF production name was
		     written in angle brackets and is not XML. -->
		<NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CS1591;CS1573</NoWarn>

		<!-- Symbols inside the assembly, as the generator carries them: a stack trace from
		     a parser here has line numbers without a symbol server. -->
		<DebugType>embedded</DebugType>

		<!-- Paths in the PDB and the package normalized to the repository rather than the
		     runner's disk, so that what CI packs can be rebuilt byte for byte from the commit
		     its nuspec names. Here and not in Directory.Build.props: it rewrites every source
		     path, [CallerFilePath] included, to /_/, and nine test classes find the
		     repository from their own file — set for everything, it failed eighty of them.
		     Only on CI, because locally it would point a debugger at /_/. -->
		<ContinuousIntegrationBuild Condition="'$(GITHUB_ACTIONS)' == 'true'">true</ContinuousIntegrationBuild>

		<!-- Generated files land on disk so a reader can open what the grammars became. -->
		<EmitCompilerGeneratedFiles>true</EmitCompilerGeneratedFiles>
		<CompilerGeneratedFilesOutputPath>$(BaseIntermediateOutputPath)GeneratedFiles</CompilerGeneratedFilesOutputPath>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<!-- The generator, and nothing else: a .gram parser acquires no runtime
		     dependency, which is why this can be an ordinary library (docs/syntax.md
		     §6.1). -->
		<ProjectReference Include="..\DotGram\DotGram.csproj"
		                  OutputItemType="Analyzer" ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup Condition="'$(DotGramAnalyzeGenerated)' == 'true'">
		<!-- The generator's own output, analysed for dead or redundant emitted code
		     (tools/DotGram.GeneratedAnalyzers), never packed, loaded only under this
		     property (docs/development.md). -->
		<ProjectReference Include="../../tools/DotGram.GeneratedAnalyzers/DotGram.GeneratedAnalyzers.csproj"
		                  OutputItemType="Analyzer" ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup Condition="'$(TargetFramework)' == 'netstandard2.0'">
		<!-- What a consumer on this framework needs: the generated parser's own methods
		     take `ReadOnlySpan<char>`, which is not in the framework there. It is the one
		     dependency this package has, and it has it only here. -->
		<PackageReference Include="System.Memory" />

		<!-- And what the compilation needs rather than the consumer: the records written
		     by hand beside the grammars have `init` accessors, and `IsExternalInit` is not
		     declared on this framework. The polyfill is a generator and emits it as an
		     internal type, so nothing reaches the package. -->
		<PackageReference Include="Meziantou.Polyfill" PrivateAssets="all" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<PropertyGroup Condition="'$(TargetFramework)' == 'netstandard2.0'">
		<!-- `required` members, which the SQL:2023 tree's records declare, need two more, and
		     SqlList<T>'s collection expressions need CollectionBuilderAttribute. -->
		<MeziantouPolyfill_IncludedPolyfills>T:System.Runtime.CompilerServices.IsExternalInit;T:System.Runtime.CompilerServices.RequiredMemberAttribute;T:System.Runtime.CompilerServices.CompilerFeatureRequiredAttribute;T:System.Runtime.CompilerServices.CollectionBuilderAttribute;T:System.Index;T:System.Range;T:System.Diagnostics.CodeAnalysis</MeziantouPolyfill_IncludedPolyfills>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<!-- What the package says for itself. The grammars are in the repository and the
		     README points at them: a parser here is worth reading as much as it is worth
		     calling. -->
		<None Include="README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />

		<!-- And the skill beside it, so that an agent in a project that has restored this
		     package can read how to use it without leaving the machine. The README says
		     where it is, in a comment nothing renders. -->
		<None Include="SKILL.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<!-- The grammars as files the generator reads: a .gram beside the class that hosts
		     it, so that another project can point the generator at the same text and compile
		     it another way — which is what DotGram.Benchmarks does with the SQL grammar. -->
		<AdditionalFiles Include="Standard\SqlStandard.gram" />
		<AdditionalFiles Include="Standard\SqlStandard92.gram" />
		<AdditionalFiles Include="TransactSql\TransactSql.gram" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<!-- For the benchmark host that compiles SqlStandard.gram on another carrier; Nodes and Towers stay internal. -->
		<InternalsVisibleTo Include="DotGram.Benchmarks" />

		<!-- For the test fixture that includes SqlStandard.gram and publishes its productions one by one
		     (tests/DotGram.Sql.Productions): the included rules call Nodes and Towers. -->
		<InternalsVisibleTo Include="DotGram.Sql.Productions" />
	</ItemGroup>

</Project>
